00:10:57.044 --> 00:12:51.324
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah, so I was about 10 and 11 years old in the mid 90s. I've just turned 40, which is old age in Scotland. We don't usually make it that long. And in the mid 90s, no camera phones, nothing like that. I used to go to the BB, which is the Boys Brigade, kind of the Scouts type organization. And it was held in a church. I wasn't and I'm not religious in any way, shape or form. I always feel I have to add that in there. And it was a kind of cold winter's night in Scotland, really busy area. A lot of the times you hear about UFOs, they're out in the desert. They're in the middle of a field somewhere, a big park. This was like all houses as far as the eye can see, shops, a busy main road, community center. And we walked out the church and there's like a long road, a couple of miles long. And I remember looking up to the right hand side to cross. And I was with my mum, my sister, her friend, my friend and his mum. And across the road to the right hand side, about half a mile away, there was like, if you could imagine a ferris wheel, but at an angle, kind of 20, 30 degrees, spinning incredibly fast. You couldn't, if I said I could make out a shape, I'd be lying. But if you imagine a saucer at an angle, but just lights going round, like if it was a ferris wheel at a carnival, everyone was dead because of the speed it was going. You couldn't discern a shape, you couldn't see the bottom of it. It was so low to the ground. So it wasn't like it was up in the sky. It was really low down. And we just watched it for a minute. I remember my mum and my friend's mum seeing how strange it looked. And we just went home from there. We lived like that way from it. So you're kind of diagonal. And yeah, mid nineties, no camera phones, no real internet to speak of at the time that people had commercially. And you don't go home and phone the police. You know, what do you do? So that was it and it always kind of stuck with me.

